---
import Layout from '../../components/Layout.astro';
import MasonXMonogram from '../../components/MasonXMonogram.astro';
import TitleBlock from '../../components/TitleBlock.astro';

const title = "Masonry Shop Drawings in SketchUp — and Why You Shouldn't | TMM Blog";
const description = "SketchUp draws boxes — it doesn't know coursing, bond, or special units. Why masonry shop drawings in SketchUp are so much hand work, and what replaces it.";
const canonical = "https://themasonrymodeler.com/blog/masonry-shop-drawings-in-sketchup/";

const blogPostingSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BlogPosting",
  "headline": "Masonry Shop Drawings in SketchUp — and Why You Shouldn't Have To",
  "description": description,
  "image": "https://themasonrymodeler.com/assets/og-image.png",
  "datePublished": "2026-06-27",
  "dateModified": "2026-06-27",
  "author": {
    "@type": "Person",
    "name": "Ryan Shanley",
    "jobTitle": "Founder",
    "worksFor": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "Powder River Masonry" },
    "url": canonical,
  },
  "publisher": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "The Masonry Modeler",
    "url": "https://themasonrymodeler.com/",
  },
  "mainEntityOfPage": { "@type": "WebPage", "@id": canonical },
};

const breadcrumbSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  "itemListElement": [
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://themasonrymodeler.com/" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Field Notes", "item": "https://themasonrymodeler.com/blog/" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": "Shop Drawings in SketchUp", "item": canonical },
  ],
};
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical}>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(blogPostingSchema)} />
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbSchema)} />
  </Fragment>

  <MasonXMonogram />
  <TitleBlock sheet="B1.7 — Shop Drawings in SketchUp" ctaLabel="Join Waitlist →" ctaHref="/#waitlist" />

  <div class="section-divider">
    <span>Field Notes · B1.7</span>
    <span class="scale-marks"></span>
    <span>Shop Drawings</span>
  </div>

  <section class="blog-post-hero">
    <div class="blog-post-hero-inner">
      <div class="post-kicker">Field Notes</div>
      <h1>
        Drawing Masonry in SketchUp — When the Model Doesn't Know It's a
        <span class="accent">Wall</span>
      </h1>
      <div class="post-meta">
        <span><strong>Ryan Shanley</strong></span>
        <span>June 2026</span>
        <span>4 min read</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="blog-post-body">

    <p>
      SketchUp is a great tool. I know guys who basically live in it. But watch somebody build a
      masonry shop drawing in SketchUp and you see the problem in about thirty seconds: they're
      drawing boxes. SketchUp doesn't know it's a wall.
    </p>

    <h2>It draws geometry, not masonry</h2>

    <p>
      A box in SketchUp is a box whether it's a CMU, a countertop, or a kitchen cabinet. The model
      has no idea what a course is. It doesn't know what bond is, that a corner needs a different
      unit than the field, that the wall has to land on an 8-inch module, or that an opening cuts
      the coursing a certain way. SketchUp will draw anything — which is exactly why it knows
      nothing about masonry specifically.
    </p>

    <p>
      So you supply all of it. By hand. Block by block. Every unit is geometry you place and align
      yourself, and the model takes your word for it because it can't tell a stretcher from a sill.
    </p>

    <h2>The grind nobody should be doing</h2>

    <p>
      Here's where the hours go. You build the wall box by box. Then the height changes — and
      nothing re-courses. SketchUp doesn't shift the bond or recut the top course, because it never
      knew there was a bond. You redo it by hand. An opening moves? You re-cut the boxes around it
      yourself. A finish band changes? You re-color geometry one face at a time.
    </p>

    <p>
      And when you're finally done, you've got a nice picture of a wall — but no takeoff that knows
      a corner from a half, because SketchUp was counting shapes, not masonry. So you're back to
      counting by hand off your own model.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        SketchUp will draw you a wall. It won't lay you one. The difference is every block that has
        to be right — and that's the part it leaves to you.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <h2>The model should know it's masonry</h2>

    <p>
      None of this is a knock on SketchUp. It's a great general modeler. It's just built to draw
      anything, and a shop drawing isn't anything — it's a coursed masonry wall that has to be
      right, course by course, before you're allowed to lay it.
    </p>

    <p>
      So build it in something that knows that. When the wall is laid course by course in a model
      that understands bond, the shop drawing and the takeoff fall out of it together — coursing
      numbered, corners closing, special units in place, halves and cuts counted, finishes banded.
      You're not modeling boxes and counting them after. You're laying a wall, and the drawing is
      already done.
    </p>

    <p>
      That's the whole idea behind <a href="/masonry-shop-drawings/">masonry shop drawings</a> in
      The Masonry Modeler: the model knows it's masonry, so you build the wall once and the
      submittal draws itself.
    </p>

    <p>
      If you're hand-building shop drawings in SketchUp before you can lay a block, that's the part
      I'm trying to take off your plate.
    </p>

    <a href="/#waitlist" class="post-cta-inline">Join the waitlist →</a>

  </article>

  <div class="blog-post-footer">
    <a href="/blog/" class="blog-back-link">← All posts</a>
  </div>

</Layout>
